titre$KEY TO THE FAMILIES OF PHANEROCHAETINEAE
com$ A new morphological arrangement of the Polyporales /I. Phanerochaetineae // adapt GF  avril 2010

clé principale

1,0$tiré de  Mycena. 2006. Vol. 6. P. 4?56. UDC 582.287.23:001.4. /// A new morphological arrangement of the Polyporales./ I. Phanerochaetineae/ © Ivan V. Zmitrovich, Vera F. Malysheva,* Wjacheslav A. Spirin**/// les numéros renvoient aux index de la revue.

1,2 $  Hyphae with simple clamp connections.
1,4 $  Hyphae as a rule without clamps in subhymenium, in some cases with scattered verticillate clamp connections in context.
2,cs2 $ Basidiospores thick-walled in various degree, ±cyanophilous. Lamprocystidia none, gloeoplerous hyphae and/or gloeocystidia usually present. Basidiocarps mostly tyromycetoid, fleshy with sarcodi(tri)mitic or pseudodi(tri)mitic context, rarely hydnoid to corticioid and monomitic. $ $s"Fam. Fistulinaceae"
2,3 $  Basidiospores thin-walled, acyanophilous in most cases. Lamprocystidia present, gloeoplerous elements absent.
3,cs1 $ Basidiocarps cantharelloid, pleurotoid, merulioid, or tyromycetoid, fleshy with monomitic or sarcodimitic context.$ $ s"Fam. Faerberiaceae"
3,0 $ Basidiocarps stereoid, often dull-coloured; hyphae sometimes without clamps. Hyphal system pseudodimitic. $ Order Hyphodermatales (Veluticeps etc. . not treated)
4,cs4 $ Basidiospores thick-walled in various degree, ±cyanophilous, if thin-walled, then context reddish in alkalis. Lamprocystidia none. Basidiocarps tyromycetoid, fleshy, or robustly porioid with sarcodimitic to pseudodimitic context. $ $s"Fam. Laetiporaceae"
4,cs3 $ Basidiospores thin-walled, acyanophilous. Lamprocystidia present or not. Basidiocarps merulioid, corticioid, delicately porioid, rarely sessile, fleshy with monomitic context. $$s" Fam. Phanerochaetaceae"



cs1,0$
1,0$ Faerberiaceae : BASIDIOCARPS orthotropic ? cantharelloid, or parageotropic to resupinate ? pleurotoid,///tyromycetoid, merulioid; fleshy; HYMENOPHORE bearing auxohymenium: lamellate, folded, or///poroid, of subgelatinous-ceraceous consistency; TRAMA consisted of parallel arranged hyphae///to irregular; CONTEXT fleshy, usually with gelatinized strata; HYPHAL SYSTEM monomitic or///sarcodimitic; HYPHAE with clamp connections; CYSTIDIA expressed as metuloids: cheilo- cylindrical///to subulate, pleuro- of tramatic or hymenial origin, fusoid, thick-walled, naked or more///often with robustly encrusted upper third; BASIDIA long-clavate, constricted, (2)4-spored;///BASIDIOSPORES ellipsoid to amygdaliform, obscurely thin-walled,6 neither amyloid nor dextrinoid,///acyanophilous. TYPE OF ROT: white.

1,0 $ Basidiocarps cantharelloid, hymenophore radiately folded. 2. $ Faerberia
1,0 $ Basidiocarps pleurotoid to resupinate, hymenophore lamellate to merulioid. 3. $ Hohenbuehelia
1,0 $ Basidiocarps sessile, fleshy, hymenophore poroid.1. $ Climacocystis


cs2,0$
Fistulinaceae Lotsy, Vortr. Bot. Stammesgesch. 1: 695, 704, 1907. .  
Sparassidaceae Herter, 1910. . Bjerkanderaceae Jülich, 1982. . Grifolaceae Jülich, 1982. .
Hapalopilaceae Jülich, 1982. . Meripilaceae Jülich, 1982.

1,0$Fistulinaceae  : BASIDIOCARPS wood-inhabiting, tyromycetoid or multipileate cantharelloid, rarely nodulose///or corticioid; HYMENOPHORE bearing auxohymenium: tubular (sometimes polycyphelloid),///spinose, tuberculate, or even, of waxy-fibrous consistency; TRAMA consisted of parallel///arranged hyphae to irregular; CONTEXT film-like or fleshy-spongious to fleshy-coriaceous;///HYPHAL SYSTEM submonomitic, sarcodi(tri)mitic, or subdi(tri)mitic; HYPHAE with regular to///irregular clamp connections, with rectangular or candelabriform branching, often inflated; gloeopleroid///elements (gliophores) present in many species; CYSTIDIA: lepto- or gloeo-, sometimes///absent. BASIDIA clavate, pedunculate, or merulioid, 4-spored; BASIDIOSPORES ellipsoid to///subglobose, mostly thick-walled, neither amyloid, nor dextrinoid, in most cases cyanophilous.///TYPE OF ROT: brown, rarely white.


1,2 $  Hymenophore smooth or irregularly tuberculate. Basidiocarps corticioid or cantharelloid multilobed.
1,4 $  Hymenophore poroid or hydnoid (with intermediates). Basidiocarps generally sessile, fleshy.
2,0 $ Basidiocarps cantharelloid multilobed, hymenophore smooth.  11. $ Sparassis
2,3 $  Basidiocarps corticioid, hymenophore tuberculate to smooth.
3,0 $ Basidia granulate. In context obligate conidial state of Sporotrichopsis type. Basidiospores angular-rugulose. 3. $ Granulobasidium
3,0 $ Basidia guttulate. Conidia in context absent. Basidiospores smooth or rugulose $ Hypochnicium (Hyphodermatales . not treated)
4,0 $ Hymenophoral tubes separated one from another, so hymenophore has polycyphelloid appearance.2. $ Fistulina
4,5 $  Hymenophore of normal polyporoid appearance, or hydnoid.
5,0 $ Basidia without a basal clamp. Mature basidiocarp enormously giant.7. $ Meripilus
5,6 $  Basidia fibulate at base. Mature basidiocarp large to small, not giant.
6,0 $ Flesh reddish-violet and spores faintly brownish in KOH.  5. $ Hapalopilus
6,7 $  Flesh unchanging or purplish-red and spores unchanging in KOH. Size of fruitbodies varying.
7,8 $  Spores elongated, walls thickening comes to light in CB-medium only.
7,10 $  Spores broadly ellipsoid to subglobose, walls mostly expressively thickened.
8,0 $ True intercalar skeletals present in context. $ Piptoporus (Polyporineae)
8,9 $  Hyphal system sarco(di)trimitic.
9,0 $ Basidiocarps fomitoid. Inflated gloeopleroid hyphae present. $ Laricifomes (Polyporineae)
9,0 $ Basidiocarps sessile, of tough-fleshy consistency. $ Osteina (Polyporineae)
10,11 $  Asexual sporulation as a rule present in situ.
10,12 $  Asexual sporulation not seen and conspicouos gloeocystidia absent.
11,0 $ Basidia less than 20 µm long, centrally constricted. Cystidia sometimes present as fusoid leptocystidia of hymenial origin. $ Oligoporus (Polyporineae)
11,0 $ Oblong hymenial gloeocystidia occuring. Basidia more than 25 µm long, stalked.  4. $ Grifola (biennis)
12,0 $ Basidia up to 20 µm in longest dimension; basidiocarps not changing in colour when brushed; spores moderately thick-walled and fairly cyanophilous; hyphae clear cyanophilous.  6. $ Loweomyces
12,13 $  Basidia longer than 20 µm or basidiocarps reddish-brown when brushed.
13,0 $ Basidiocarps robustly sessile, fleshy, flesh often changing in colour in KOH, or reddish-brown when bruised.  1. $ Aurantioporus
13,14 $  Flesh not changing in colour in KOH or when bruised.
14,0 $ Spores thick-walled, strongly cyanophilous.  10. $ Sarcodontia
14,15 $  Spores with slightly thickened walls, reaction in CB various.
15,0 $ Basidiocarps mostly multipileate; when resupinate on underside of logs, then with black zone above tube layer.  4. $ Grifola
15,16 $  Basidiocarps normally resupinate, hymenophore hydnoid or poroid; black zone absent.
16,0 $ Cystidial elements absent. Hymenophore poroid.  8. $ Raduliporus
16,0 $ Gloeocystidia often present. Hymenophore hydnoid.  9. $ Radulodon


 cs3,0$

Phanerochaetaceae (J. Erikss.) Jülich, Bibl. Mycol. 85: 384, 1982. . Corticiaceae
subfam. Phanerochaetoideae (J. Erikss. per Jülich) Parmasto, 1986. . Climacodontaceae
Jülich, 1982.

1,0$Phanerochaetaceae : BASIDIOCARPS wood inhabiting or lichenized, tyromycetoid, stereoid, merulioid, porioid,///odontioid, corticioid (often rhizomoid); HYMENOPHORE bearing auxo- or leptohymenium:///folded, tubular, spinose, tuberculate to even, of ceraceous consistency; TRAMA consisted of///parallel arranged hyphae to irregular; CONTEXT film-like, waxy or fleshy without gelatinized///strata; HYPHAL SYSTEM monomitic, submonomitic, pseudodimitic; HYPHAE simple septate or///with verticillate clamp connections (except of Porostereum and some Climacodon species with///irregularly clamped hymenial hyphae), with athelioid, rectangular, or candelabriform branching,///often inflated towards the septa; CYSTIDIA: lepto- or lampro-, sometimes absent. BASIDIA///clavate to long-clavate or merulioid, 4-spored; BASIDIOSPORES ellipsoid, cylindrical, amygdaliform,///or suballantoid, rather thin-walled, neither amyloid, nor dextrinoid, cyanophilous reaction///varies. TYPE OF ROT: white, as exception brown (Leptoporus mollis, Ceraceomerulius albostramineus).

1,2 $  Subhymenial hyphae regularly or irregularly clamped.
1,3 $  Subhymenial hyphae without clamps; basidiocarps varying in shape and sizes, but hymenial surface not hydnoid.
2,0 $ Basidiocarps stereoid.15. $ Porostereum
2,0 $ Basidiocarps tyromycetoid with toothed hymenophore, sessile or imbricate 3. $ Climacodon
3,4 $  Lamprocystidia present.
3,5 $  With pseudo- or leptocystidia, or without them.
4,0 $ Genetartive hyphae with normal lumen, pseudoskeletal hyphae with narrow lumen; basidiocarps stereoid 8 $ Hjortstamia
4,0 $ Hyphae with narrow lumen; basidiocarps merulioid to corticioid. 1. $ Australohydnum
4,0 $ Hyphae with normal lumen; basidiocarps corticioid.14. $ Phanerochaete
5,6 $  Hymenophore truly poroid.
5,7 $  Hymenophore smooth to odontioid. Basidiocarps resupinate.
6,0 $ Basidiocarps porioid (one species merulioid infundibuliform), only basal hyphae with thickened walls, sometimes clamped. Spores allantoid to cylindrical; chlamydospores and leptocystidia absent.6. $ Gloeoporus
6,0 $ Basidiocarps sessile, fleshy, hyphae thick-walled, always without clamps. 12. $ Leptoporus
6,0 $ Basidiocarps with large, often irregular pores. Leptocystidia present. Spores ellipsoid or cylindrical (if so, chlamydospores present). 5. $ Emmia
7,8 $  Basidia up to 20 µm long.
7,9 $  Basidia longer than 20 µm.
8,0 $ Basidia thick-walled at base, with pseudocystidia. 13. $ Odonticium
8,0 $ Basidia thin-walled at base, with leptocystidia.9. $ Hydnophlebia
9,0 $ Hymenophore grandinioid, aculei tips with crystalline encrustation; true cystidia absent. 10. $ Hyphodermella
9,10 $  Hymenophore grandinioid in some species, aculei tips without encrustation.Cystidia present or absent.
10,0 $ With pseudocystidia; basal hyphae grossly thick-walled and candelabriform in subiculum. 7. $ Grandiniella
10,11 $  Cystidia lacking or present as leptocystidia; basal hyphae thin- to moderately thick-walled, branched at acute to right angles.
11,0 $ Hymenium pellicular (leptohymenium); tissue loose, leptocystidia usually present.11 $ Leptochaete
11,12 $  Hymenium firm waxy (auxohymenium); leptocystidia absent or indistinct.
12,0 $ Spores cylindrical to amygdaliform; contextual hyphae not wider than epibasidial segment. 2. $ Byssomerulius
12,0 $ Spores ellipsoid; contextual hyphae wider than epibasidial segment. 4. $ Efibula


